Hello,

After some time reading docs and other scripts I decided to write my=20
very own lvm2create_initrd.

Inspiration to write this script came from various sources
1. The original LVM lvmcreate_initrd [1]
This is the script I started with

2. Kernel Documentation/initrd.txt [2]
This is where I learned the two mechanisms to change the root=20
filesystem: change_root and pivot_root

3. EVMS INSTALL.initrd & linuxrc [3]
Some ideas like greping the /proc/cmdline for lvm2root parameter

4. Jeffrey Layton's lvm2create_initrd: [4]
Some ideas like lvm2rescue and more

5. Christophe Saout's initrd & linuxrc: [5]
Great linuxrc. However it still uses the deprecated change_root=20
mechanism and devfs

This script tries to be the combination of the best features of all=20
sources and implements a pure pivot_root mechanism to change the root=20
filesystem.
To use the initrd generated by this script you have to pass the kernel=20
some parameters:
    root=3D/dev/ram0=20
       (mandatory) This is needed for the kernel to treat the initrd=20
ramdisk as root and execute /sbin/init with PID 1 as opposed to starting=20
/linuxrc with pid !=3D1. This is necessary for the final part of the script.
    lvm2root=3D/dev/VG/LV
       (mandatory) lvm2root is the name of your LV root volume, for=20
example /dev/system/root
    lvm2rescue
       (optional) you can pass this option to have access to a shell=20
inside initrd

If you use lilo try adding/modifying an entry similar to this one in=20
lilo.conf:

image=3D/boot/vmlinuz-lvm2-2.6.3-4
        label=3D"ramdisk_LVM"
        initrd=3D/boot/initrd-lvm2-2.6.3-4.gz
        append=3D"root=3D/dev/ram0 lvm2root=3D/dev/system/root <other param=
eters>"

If using grub try adding/modifying an entry similar to this one in menu.lst

title ramdisk LVM
        kernel /boot/vmlinuz-lvm2-2.6.3-4 root=3D/dev/ram0=20
lvm2root=3D/dev/system/root <other parameters>
        initrd /boot/initrd-lvm2-2.6.3-4.gz


This script has only been tested on Debian sid (unstable) with kernel=20
2.6 with everything required to boot
the root filesystem built-in (not as modules). Ex: SCSI or IDE, RAID,=20
device mapper
It does not support modules nor devfs as devfs is deprecated in the 2.6=20
kernel series
It needs lvm2 tools, busybox, pivot_root, MAKEDEV

Comments are welcome

Have fun

